# Flutter — Checkbox, Radio & Switch

These widgets allow users to **select, enable, or disable options**.

---

# 1. Checkbox

A checkbox is used when the user can select **multiple independent options**.

Example:

```text id="x5w8kj"
☑ Java
☐ Python
☑ Flutter
```

The user can select both Java **and** Flutter.

### Basic code

```dart id="t2q3m8"
bool isChecked = false;

Checkbox(
  value: isChecked,

  onChanged: (value) {
    setState(() {
      isChecked = value!;
    });
  },
)
```

---

# 2. Checkbox with Text

Usually we put it inside a `Row`:

```dart id="7r4g0d"
Row(
  children: [

    Checkbox(
      value: isChecked,

      onChanged: (value) {
        setState(() {
          isChecked = value!;
        });
      },
    ),

    Text("I agree to the terms"),
  ],
)
```

Common use:

```text id="d1y6a8"
☑ I agree to Terms & Conditions
☑ Remember me
☑ Receive notifications
```

---

# 3. CheckboxListTile

For a cleaner layout, Flutter provides:

```dart id="q0g4ne"
CheckboxListTile(
  title: Text("Remember me"),

  value: isChecked,

  onChanged: (value) {
    setState(() {
      isChecked = value!;
    });
  },
)
```

This automatically creates a row with the checkbox and text.

---

# 4. Radio

A Radio button is used when the user must select **one option from a group**.

Example:

```text id="1j8c6r"
○ Male
● Female
○ Other
```

Only one option can be selected.

### Basic idea

```dart id="l4z7a2"
String gender = "Male";
```

Then:

```dart id="u4z1hc"
Radio<String>(
  value: "Male",
  groupValue: gender,

  onChanged: (value) {
    setState(() {
      gender = value!;
    });
  },
)
```

Another option:

```dart id="x1t7w0"
Radio<String>(
  value: "Female",
  groupValue: gender,

  onChanged: (value) {
    setState(() {
      gender = value!;
    });
  },
)
```

Because both use the same:

```dart
groupValue: gender
```

only one can be selected.

---

# 5. RadioListTile

Instead of manually creating a Row:

```dart id="a9e8v4"
RadioListTile<String>(
  title: Text("Male"),

  value: "Male",

  groupValue: gender,

  onChanged: (value) {
    setState(() {
      gender = value!;
    });
  },
)
```

Another:

```dart id="n5g2e1"
RadioListTile<String>(
  title: Text("Female"),

  value: "Female",

  groupValue: gender,

  onChanged: (value) {
    setState(() {
      gender = value!;
    });
  },
)
```

---

# 6. Switch

A switch is used for **on/off settings**.

Example:

```text id="z8k3qt"
Notifications     [ ON ]
Dark Mode          [ OFF ]
Wi-Fi              [ ON ]
```

### Basic code

```dart id="v6f2y8"
bool notifications = false;

Switch(
  value: notifications,

  onChanged: (value) {
    setState(() {
      notifications = value;
    });
  },
)
```

---

# 7. SwitchListTile

This is commonly used for settings screens:

```dart id="4s6d8p"
SwitchListTile(
  title: Text("Notifications"),

  value: notifications,

  onChanged: (value) {
    setState(() {
      notifications = value;
    });
  },
)
```

Example settings screen:

```text id="t8q4k2"
Settings

Notifications          [ ON ]

Dark Mode              [ OFF ]

Auto Download          [ ON ]
```

---

# 8. Main Difference

This is the most important thing to remember:

| Widget     | Purpose             | Selection |
| ---------- | ------------------- | --------- |
| `Checkbox` | Independent option  | Multiple  |
| `Radio`    | Choose one option   | One       |
| `Switch`   | Turn setting on/off | On / Off  |

### Easy memory

```text id="r5b8w1"
Checkbox
   ↓
Can select MANY

Radio
   ↓
Select ONE from a group

Switch
   ↓
ON / OFF
```

